{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7K37NiPW2vsVPetMc7v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<final_document>\n[Date]\n\nDear Members of the Admissions Committee,\n\nI am writing to recommend Priya [Last Name] for doctoral study in marine ecology. I have taught biology at [College] for 17 years and supervised about 30 senior theses. Priya is one of the three best students I have taught in that time.\n\nI know her work well. She took three courses with me, and in two of them she earned the top grade in a class of 28. Her senior thesis, which I supervised, gave me the clearest view of what she can do as a researcher.\n\nFor her thesis, Priya compared oyster growth at six sites in our bay over fourteen months. She did all the fieldwork herself, which came to about ninety trips by kayak in every season. Field projects at this scale usually fall apart for undergraduates. Hers didn't, because she kept going back. She found faster growth at the two sites nearest the salt marsh, which is an interesting result. To be accurate, this is one season of data. It has not been repeated, and a storm cost one site three months of records. With a second season, I expect this work to be publishable. Priya has already presented it as a poster at a regional conference, where she discussed it with researchers outside our college.\n\nHer work outside the classroom points the same way. Last summer she worked as a paid field assistant at a state shellfish lab. For two weeks she ran the lab's water sampling program alone. Her supervisor there told me he would hire her again without hesitation. Working scientists have trusted her with real responsibility, and she has handled it.\n\nGraduate study in marine ecology demands long, repetitive fieldwork, careful data collection, and the persistence to see a project through. Priya has already shown all three in the field, not just in class. I recommend her without reservation and expect her to do excellent doctoral work.\n\nPlease feel free to contact me if I can tell you more.\n\nSincerely,\n\n[Your Name]\n[Title], Department of Biology\n[College]\n[Email] | [Phone]\n</final_document>\n\nI used the \"publishable with a second season\" wording you agreed to. I also mentioned the storm gap in one clause. Saying it yourself makes the rest of the letter more believable, and it reads as a strength (she was honest about her data) rather than a flaw. You can cut that clause if you'd rather leave it out.\n\nA few things to fill in or check:\n- **Placeholders:** her last name, the date and your signature block.\n- **\"Without hesitation\":** you told me her supervisor said he'd hire her again, and I added \"without hesitation.\" Remove those two words if that goes beyond what he actually said.\n- **Course names:** if you add the names of the courses where she earned the top grade, the letter gets more specific and still fits on one page."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1019,"cache_read_input_tokens":9800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1019},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01b50cc9-52ac-42ec-b3f5-e83f96c8958e","uuid":"957da152-8920-4df8-8410-a488c858971a","timestamp":"2026-11-16T21:24:37.390Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7K3795GJAN3DTQQiksu"}
